## Radical Innovation (advisory, not applied)
The recurring wall here (and in the archived windows-compat-audit) is "no clone-portable argv0 that both `cmd.exe` and a Unix shell will exec." A batch/shell polyglot launcher sidesteps that instead of picking a host-specific string: ship `.summem/summem.cmd` whose first bytes are a `cmd.exe`-legal comment that also parses as a POSIX no-op, followed by a line `cmd.exe` executes as `@python %~f0 %*`-style self-invocation and POSIX shells execute as `exec python3 "$0" "$@"`. `AGENTS.md`'s bootstrap line then never needs to know the host at all — one literal command, no `python` prefix, no conditional — because the same bytes are valid input to both interpreters. This would let `prompt_text()` collapse back to a single unconditional invocation instead of "always prefix with python," while still fixing the WinError 193 problem at its root (process start), not by asking the agent to supply an interpreter. Recorded as advisory only; do not implement as part of this task.

